      "filename": "Sargent Antonio Bennett 3rd - INSCOM Journal September 1991 Pg 11.png",
      "title": "File:Sargent Antonio Bennett 3rd - INSCOM Journal September 1991 Pg 11.png",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Sargent_Antonio_Bennett_3rd_-_INSCOM_Journal_September_1991_Pg_11.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargent_Antonio_Bennett_3rd_-_INSCOM_Journal_September_1991_Pg_11.png",
      "width": 700,
      "height": 900,
      "mime": "image/png",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "U.S. Army",
      "credit": "U.S.Army Intelligence &amp; Security Command INSCOM Journal, September 1991",
      "source": "Sargent Antonio Bennett 3rd - INSCOM Journal September 1991 Pg 11",
      "description": "Intelligence &amp; Security Command magazine INSCOM Journal September 1991 Pg 11. Dinner at Tuefelsberg Mess hall honoring Field Station Berlin FSB Soldiers returning from Persian Gulf. \nSargent Bennett, previously the Berlin Brigade Terrorism Analyst, served as Order of Battle Analyst for FSB. \nHe volunteered for and was deployed during Desert Shield with elements of the Berlin BDE's 766th Military Intelligence Detachments Imagery Analysts (96D). \nWeeks before Desert Storm, Army Central Command ARCENT Forward HQ was relocated from Riyadh to Eskan Village, 60 kilometres South. Bennett became Night Shift NCOIC of Classified Courier Team CCT. The CCT mission was to deliver all classified documents &amp; support  40th Engineer Topographic Battalion of the 82nd Airborne in the creation and distribution of maps developed in part by 766MID. All CANUKUS National Level Intelligence Assets and IMINT used by Coalition Forces was distributed &amp; dissiminated by this team. Sargent Bennett personally delivered Intelligence Briefs &amp; Reports to General Schwartkopf and his staff located within the Saudi Oil Ministry. \n\nSargent Thompson, Curry, &amp; Thalacker, Signals Analysts and Polish, Russian Linguists (98C-G), arrived after the Desert Storm began, and were attached to elements of the 513 MI Battalion, Ft. Monmouth.",
      "dateCreated": "1991-09-01",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "teufelsberg-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u336",
      "smallPath": "_m/u/3/3/6/teufelsberg-wp/intelligence-amp-secur-small.webp"
    },
